MAPS 

- In Go maps is a collection of key value pairs 

- Its simiar to Hash in Ruby , objects in JS and Dict in Python


- In maps both the keys and values are statically typed - keys and values are to be of same type

                     MAPS
KEYS - same type               VALUES - same type


DECLARATION and some built in functions

package main

import "fmt"

func main() {
	//here we are declaring maps which have type string and all the values are also of type string

	//1st way of declaring maps
	// colors := map[string]string{
	// 	"red":   "#ff0000",
	// 	"green": "#4bf75e",
	// }

	//2nd way of declaration - we declare it via this method after we want to figue out what values we want to add to it
	// var colors map[string]string

	//3rd method of declaration
	colors := make(map[string]string)

	//if we want to add new values to map

	//here white is the key and fffff is the value
	colors["white"] = "#ffffff"
	//we donot get access to dot operator like we can do with structs and the reason for this is because all the keys inside the map are typed

	//delete values from map
	delete(colors, "white")

	fmt.Println(colors)
}



/////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////


ITERATION and other built in functions

Iteration over maps is very similar to slices 

package main

import "fmt"

func main() {

	colors := map[string]string{
		"red":   "#ff0000",
		"green": "#4bf75e",
		"white": "#ffffff",
	}

	// fmt.Println(colors)
	printMap(colors)
}

//we want to write a new function that accepts a map and iterate over the map and print out all the key values pairs

func printMap(c map[string]string) {
	for color, hex := range c {
		fmt.Println("Hex code for", color, "is", hex)
	}
}


/////////////////////////////////////////////////////

DIFFERENCE between maps and struts

maps 
- all keys must of the same type
- use to repesent a collection of related properties
- all values must be of the same type
- Dont need to know all the keys at the compile time
- Keys are indexed we can iterate over them
- Reference type!

structs
- values can of different type
- You need to know all the different fields at the compile time
- Keys dont support indexing 
- Use to represent a "thing" with a lot of different properties
- Value Type!